# DESCRIPTION

A VMO (virtual memory object) is a run of pages that a process maps into its address space with vmar_map(2), or reads and writes by operations on it.
Its size is a whole number of pages.
The pages of an ordinary VMO are zero-filled and committed when first touched.
A VMO has a content size, a byte count no larger than its size, that object_get_property(2) reads and writes.
The signal `CROI_VMO_ZERO_CHILDREN` is asserted while the VMO has no children; a child clears it, and it is set again once the last child is gone, which is after the child's last handle and last mapping.

## vmo_create

`vmo_create` writes a handle to a new VMO of <size> bytes to <out>.
<size> is rounded up to a page; if it was not a multiple of a page, it is the content size.
The handle has TRANSFER, DUPLICATE, WAIT, INSPECT, READ, WRITE, MAP, GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY and SIGNAL.
<options> is any of:

: `CROI_VMO_CREATE_RESIZABLE`
  vmo_set_size may change its size, and the handle also has RESIZE.
: `CROI_VMO_CREATE_DISCARDABLE`
  The kernel may take the pages back while the VMO is unlocked; see vmo_op_range below.
: `CROI_VMO_CREATE_UNBOUNDED`
  The VMO is as large as a VMO can be, and <size> is its content size.
  It cannot be combined with RESIZABLE (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).
: `CROI_VMO_CHARGE_TO`
  The pages are charged to the account whose handle is the fourth argument, <account>, which needs CHARGE.

Without CHARGE_TO the pages are charged to the account of the calling process, if it has one; account(2).
A size too large is `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`; any other option is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The job's policy for new VMOs applies.

## vmo_create_child

`vmo_create_child` writes a handle to a child of <vmo> to <out>.
The handle <vmo> needs DUPLICATE and READ.
<size> is rounded up to a page, and as in vmo_create a size that is not a multiple of a page is the child's content size.
<options> picks one kind, with any of RESIZABLE and NO_WRITE as far as the kind allows:

: `CROI_VMO_CHILD_SNAPSHOT`, `CROI_VMO_CHILD_SNAPSHOT_AT_LEAST_ON_WRITE`, `CROI_VMO_CHILD_SNAPSHOT_MODIFIED`
  A copy-on-write snapshot of [<offset>, <offset> + <size>) of the parent.
  The first is isolated from the parent both ways; the second copies each page in at the child's first touch of it.
  Not under a pager VMO for the first.
: `CROI_VMO_CHILD_SLICE`
  The same pages, at an offset, page aligned and wholly inside the parent (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS` otherwise).
  It may be of an anonymous VMO that is not resizable, or of a physical or contiguous one, with the parent's cache policy.
  A slice of a slice is a slice of the root.
  It must be given alone.
: `CROI_VMO_CHILD_REFERENCE`
  Another handle-able name for the parent itself, with <offset> and <size> 0.
  `CROI_VMO_CHILD_RESIZABLE` with it needs RESIZE on the parent's handle and the parent resizable.
  A physical VMO has no reference (`CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`).

`CROI_VMO_CHILD_NO_WRITE` with RESIZABLE is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
A discardable parent is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.
A kind not listed, or two kinds at once, is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The child's handle has the rights of <vmo>'s less RESIZE, plus RESIZE for a RESIZABLE child, plus GET_PROPERTY and SET_PROPERTY.
NO_WRITE also removes WRITE; without it a snapshot gains WRITE and loses EXECUTE, which is how a loader keeps code executable.
A snapshot is charged to the calling process's account, or with `CROI_VMO_CHARGE_TO` to the account in <account>.
A slice of a physical or contiguous VMO with CHARGE_TO is charged its whole size and never evicted, which is how device-local memory is budgeted.
A reference owns no pages and takes none.

## vmo_op_range

`vmo_op_range` does <op> on [<offset>, <offset> + <size>) of the VMO.
A range beyond the VMO is `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`.
Commit, prefetch and the hints round the range out to pages; the others work on the bytes given.

| op | Right | Does |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_COMMIT` | WRITE | commits the pages, reading them from the pager first if there is one |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_DECOMMIT` | WRITE | frees the pages; <offset> and <size> page aligned. Anonymous VMOs free them; a contiguous VMO lends them to the system; a physical one is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_ZERO` | WRITE | zeroes the bytes; `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` for a physical VMO |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_PREFETCH` | READ | asks the pager for the pages; no effect on a VMO no pager backs |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_CACHE_SYNC` | READ | makes written code visible to instruction fetch; <size> not 0 |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_CACHE_CLEAN` | READ | writes cached lines back; <size> not 0 |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_CACHE_CLEAN_INVALIDATE` | READ | writes back and drops cached lines; <size> not 0 |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_CACHE_INVALIDATE` | | `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_LOCK` | READ or WRITE | locks a discardable VMO, all of it; writes a `croi_vmo_lock_state_t` to <buffer> (<buffer_size> at least its 32 bytes) whose `discarded_size` is the VMO's size if the kernel had taken its pages, else 0 |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_TRY_LOCK` | READ or WRITE | as LOCK but `CROI_ERR_UNAVAILABLE` if the pages were taken; writes nothing |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_UNLOCK` | READ or WRITE | unlocks; `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` if it was not locked |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_DONT_NEED` | none | hints that a pager VMO's pages may be evicted first |
| `CROI_VMO_OP_ALWAYS_NEED` | none | hints that a pager VMO's pages should stay |

The lock operations are `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` on a VMO that is not discardable, and need <offset> 0 and <size> the VMO's size (`CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`).
While a discardable VMO is locked the kernel keeps its pages.
Once taken, they are gone, every access fails with `CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND` and a LOCK is how a program learns it.
The hints are ignored on a VMO no pager backs.
An op not listed is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
A missing right is `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`.

## vmo_set_cache_policy

`vmo_set_cache_policy` sets the memory type later mappings of the VMO use.
The handle needs MAP.
<policy> is `CROI_CACHE_POLICY_CACHED`, `CROI_CACHE_POLICY_UNCACHED` or `CROI_CACHE_POLICY_UNCACHED_DEVICE` (the last two both give device memory) or `CROI_CACHE_POLICY_WRITE_COMBINING`; another is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
Committed pages are cleaned and invalidated first.
It fails with `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` while the VMO is mapped or pinned, has children, is a slice or reference, or is backed by a pager or discardable (these stay cached).
Memory the firmware owns can only be cached, and a change is `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`.

## vmo_create_physical

`vmo_create_physical` writes to <out> a handle to a VMO of the physical range [<paddr>, <paddr> + <size>).
<mmio> is a resource(2) of kind MMIO that grants the range, <paddr> and <size> are page aligned and <size> is not 0.
The VMO is uncached device memory, with the rights of vmo_create's handle.
RAM and the registers the kernel uses itself cannot be granted: they are `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`.
The job's policy for new VMOs applies.

## vmo_replace_as_executable

`vmo_replace_as_executable` consumes the handle <vmo>, whether or not it succeeds, and writes to <out> a handle to the same VMO with EXECUTE added.
<vmex> is a resource(2) that grants the VMEX system resource.
Without EXECUTE a VMO cannot be mapped executable.

## vmo_set_size

`vmo_set_size` changes the size of a resizable anonymous VMO to <size>, rounded up to a page.
The handle needs WRITE and RESIZE.
The content size becomes <size> exactly, and the bytes between it and the end of its last page read as zero.
It fails with `CROI_ERR_UNAVAILABLE` for a VMO that is not resizable or is physical or contiguous, `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` for a size too large, and `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` when pinned pages would be cut off.

